: Content may be subject to copyright.\nL46: \nL47: Proof of a Conjecture of Proctor and Scoppetta Related to d-Complete Pose\nL48: \nL49: ts.pdf\nL50: Available via license: 【11†CC BY-NC 4.0】\nL51: \nL52: Content may be subject to copyright.\nL53: \nL54: Mathematica Pannonica\nL55: \nL56: N ew Series\nL57: \nL58: 28 /NS 2/ (2022) 1, 69-75\nL59: \nL60: DOI: 10.1556/314.2022.00009\nL61: \nL62: PROOF OF A CONJECTURE OF PROCTOR AND SCOPPETTA\nL63: \nL64: RELATED TO 𝑑-COMPLETE POSETS\nL65: \nL66: Jonathan David Farley 1,∗\nL67: \nL68: 1\nL69: \nL70: Department of Mathematics, Morgan State University, 1700 E. Cold Spring Lane, Baltimore, MD 21251, United\nL71: \nL72: States of America\nL73: \nL74: Communicated by Vedran Krčadinac\nL75: \nL76: Original Research Paper\nL77: \nL78: Received: Oct 19, 2021\nL79: \nL80: \nL81: Accepted: Jan 30, 2022\nL82: \nL83: First published online: Mar 9, 2022\nL84: \nL85: ©\nL86: \nL87: 2022 The Author(s)\nL88: \nL89: ABSTRACT\nL90: \nL91: Proctor and Scoppetta conjectured that\nL92: \nL93: (1) there exists an innite locally nite poset that satises their conditions VT and NTC but not SIS;\nL94: \nL95: (2) there exists an innite locally nite poset satisfying their conditions D3−C and D3MF but not both VT and FT; and\nL96: \nL97: (3) there exists an innite locally nite poset satisfying their conditions D3−C and D3MD but not NCC.\nL98: In this note, the conjecture of Proctor and Scoppetta, which is related to 𝑑-complete posets, is proven.\nL99: \nL100: KEYWORDS\nL101: \nL102: (Partially) ordered set, 𝒅-complete poset\nL103: \nL104: MATHEMATICS SUBJECT CLASSIFICATION (2020)\nL105: \nL106: Primary 06A07, 06A12\nL107: \nL108: 1. DEFINITIONS\nL109: \nL110: The terminology largely (but not entirely) comes from [\nL111: \nL112: 4\nL113: \nL114: ], [\nL115: \nL116: 2\nL117: \nL118: ], and [\nL119: \nL120: 1\nL121: \nL122: ]. Those seeking the motivation\nL123: \nL124: for the properties considered and the conjecture can read [4].\nL125: \nL126: Let\nL127: \nL128: 𝑃\nL129: be a poset. If we are dealing with more than one poset, we may use a subscript to indicate\nL130: \nL131: which poset we are considering. Let\nL132: \nL133: 𝑝, 𝑞, 𝑟, 𝑠 ∈𝑃\nL134: \nL135: . Let\nL136: \nL137: 𝑄, 𝑅 ⊆ 𝑃\nL138: \nL139: . By\nL140: \nL141: ↓𝑅 𝑄\nL142: \nL143: we mean\nL144: \nL145: {𝑠∈𝑅∣\nL146: \nL147: for some 𝑞∈𝑄, 𝑠 ≤𝑞}\nL148: \nL149: ;\nL150: \nL151: ↓𝑄\nL152: \nL153: means\nL154: \nL155: ↓𝑃 𝑄\nL156: \nL157: and\nL158: \nL159: ↓𝑅 𝑝\nL160: \nL161: means\nL162: \nL163: ↓𝑅{𝑝}\nL164: \nL165: . If\nL166: \nL167: 𝑝≤𝑞\nL168: \nL169: , then the interval\nL170: \nL171: [𝑝, 𝑞]\nL172: \nL173: is\nL174: \nL175: {𝑡∈𝑃∣𝑝≤𝑡≤𝑞}. A poset is locally nite if every interval is nite.\nL176: \nL177: We say\nL178: \nL179: 𝑞\nL180: \nL181: is a cover of\nL182: \nL183: 𝑝\nL184: \nL185: if\nL186: \nL187: 𝑝 < 𝑞\nL188: \nL189: and\nL190: \nL191: |[𝑝, 𝑞]|= 2\nL192: \nL193: ; we denote this by\nL194: \nL195: 𝑝→𝑞\nL196: \nL197: . The notation\nL198: \nL199: 𝑝→{𝑞, 𝑟 }\nL200: \nL201: means\nL202: \nL203: 𝑝→𝑞\nL204: \nL205: ,\nL206: \nL207: 𝑝→𝑟\nL208: \nL209: , and\nL210: \nL211: 𝑞≠𝑟\nL212: ; in this case,\nL213: \nL214: {𝑝, 𝑞, 𝑟}\nL215: \nL216: is a\nL217: \nL218: d−\nL219: \nL220: 3\nL221: \nL222: -set;\nL223: \nL224: {𝑞, 𝑟 }→𝑝\nL225: \nL226: means\nL227: \nL228: 𝑞→𝑝\nL229: \nL230: ,\nL231: \nL232: 𝑟→𝑝\nL233: \nL234: ,\nL235: \nL236: and 𝑞≠𝑟.\nL237: \nL238: A chain in\nL239: \nL240: 𝑃\nL241: \nL242: is a subset\nL243: \nL244: 𝐶\nL245: \nL246: such that, for all\nL247: \nL248: 𝑐, 𝑑 ∈𝐶\nL249: \nL250: , we have\nL251: \nL252: 𝑐≤𝑑\nL253: \nL254: or\nL255: \nL256: 𝑑≤𝑐\nL257: \nL258: . If\nL259: \nL260: ↓𝑝\nL261: \nL262: is nite, the\nL263: \nL264: rank of 𝑝,h(𝑝), is\nL265: \nL266: max{|𝐶|− 1 ∣ 𝐶 is a chain in ↓𝑝}.\nL267: \nL268: * Corresponding author. E-mail: lattice.theory@gmail.com\nL269: \nL270: Unauthenticated | Downloaded 05/12/22 04:58 AM UTC\nL271: \nL272: 7 0\nL273: \nL274: Mathematica Pannonica\nL275: \nL276: N ew Series\nL277: \nL278: 28 /NS 2/ (2022) 1, 69-75\nL279: \nL280: We say a non-empty poset\nL281: \nL282: 𝑃\nL283: \nL284: is ranked if, for all\nL285: \nL286: 𝑡∈𝑃\nL287: \nL288: ,\nL289: \nL290: ↓𝑡\nL291: is nite and every maximal chain in\nL292: \nL293: ↓𝑡\nL294: \nL295: has the same cardinality; the rank of\nL296: \nL297: 𝑃\nL298: \nL299: is then the maximum rank of an element (if that maximum\nL300: \nL301: exists).\nL302: \nL303: The poset\nL304: \nL305: 𝑃\nL306: \nL307: satises VT if\nL308: \nL309: 𝑝→{𝑞, 𝑟 }\nL310: \nL311: implies\nL312: \nL313: {𝑞, 𝑟 }→𝑡\nL314: \nL315: for some\nL316: \nL317: 𝑡∈𝑃\nL318: \nL319: . The poset\nL320: \nL321: 𝑃\nL322: \nL323: satises\nL324: \nL325: NTC if no element of 𝑃 has three (or more) distinct covers.\nL326: \nL327: The poset\nL328: \nL329: 𝑃\nL330: \nL331: satises SIS if, whenever\nL332: \nL333: [𝑝, 𝑞]\nL334: \nL335: is an interval and there exists\nL336: \nL337: 𝑡∈𝑃\nL338: \nL339: such that\nL340: \nL341: 𝑝→𝑡\nL342: \nL343: and 𝑡→𝑞, we have |[𝑝, 𝑞]|∈ {3,4}.\nL344: \nL345: A diamond in\nL346: \nL347: 𝑃\nL348: \nL349: is a subset\nL350: \nL351: {𝑝, 𝑞, 𝑟, 𝑠}\nL352: \nL353: such that\nL354: 𝑝→{𝑞, 𝑟 }\nL355: \nL356: and\nL357: \nL358: {𝑞, 𝑟 }→𝑠\nL359: \nL360: ; the elbows are\nL361: \nL362: 𝑞\nL363: \nL364: and 𝑟. An interval [𝑝, 𝑠 ]is a d 3-interval if it is a diamond.\nL365: \nL366: The poset\nL367: \nL368: 𝑃\nL369: \nL370: has property D3\nL371: \nL372: −\nL373: \nL374: C if, for every\nL375: \nL376: 𝑑−\nL377: \nL378: 3\nL379: \nL380: -set\nL381: \nL382: 𝑆\nL383: \nL384: , there exists\nL385: \nL386: 𝑡∈𝑃\nL387: \nL388: such that\nL389: \nL390: 𝑆∪ {𝑡}\nL391: \nL392: is a\nL393: \nL394: 𝑑 3-interval.\nL395: \nL396: A poset has the property D3MF if the top of each\nL397: \nL398: 𝑑 3\nL399: \nL400: -interval only covers the elbows of that\nL401: \nL402: interval.\nL403: \nL404: A poset satises FT if the top of each diamond only covers the elbows of that diamond.\nL405: \nL406: A poset satises D3MD if distinct 𝑑 3-intervals have distinct tops.\nL407: A poset satises NCC if, for all\nL408: \nL409: 𝑡, 𝑢, 𝑣, 𝑤 ∈𝑃\nL410: \nL411: , we do not have\nL412: \nL413: 𝑡→{𝑣, 𝑤 }\nL414: \nL415: and\nL416: \nL417: 𝑢→{𝑣, 𝑤 }\nL418: \nL419: with\nL420: \nL421: 𝑡≠𝑢.\nL422: \nL423: 2. PRIOR RESULTS AND THE CONJECTURE OF PROCTOR AND SCOPPETTA\nL424: \nL425: Proctor and Scoppetta proved [4, Theorem 4.4(c), (e), and part of (f)]:\nL426: \nL427: THEOREM 2.1. Let 𝑃 be a nite poset. Then:\nL428: \nL429: (1) VT and NTC imply SIS;\nL430: \nL431: (2) D3−C and D3MF imply VT and FT; and\nL432: \nL433: (3) D3−C and D3MD imply NCC. □\nL434: \nL435: Proctor and Scoppetta conjectured [4, Conjecture 4.5]:\nL436: \nL437: CONJECTURE 2.2.\nL438: There exist innite, locally nite posets that are counterexamples to (1), (2), and (3)\nL439: \nL440: if we drop the assumption of niteness. □\nL441: \nL442: 3. THE FIRST PART OF THE CONJECTURE OF PROCTOR AND SCOPPETTA\nL443: \nL444: PROPOSITION 3.1.\nL445: \nL446: Let\nL447: \nL448: 𝑃\nL449: \nL450: be the poset of Figure 1: it is the disjoint union of\nL451: \nL452: {𝑎 0, 𝑎 1, 𝑎 2,… }\nL453: \nL454: ,\nL455: \nL456: {𝑏 1, 𝑏 2, 𝑏 3,… }\nL457: \nL458: ,\nL459: \nL460: and\nL461: \nL462: {Fred}\nL463: \nL464: , where\nL465: \nL466: Fred →𝑎 0→𝑎 1→𝑎 2→ ⋯\nL467: \nL468: ;\nL469: \nL470: Fred →𝑏 1→𝑏 2→𝑏 3→ ⋯\nL471: \nL472: ; for\nL473: \nL474: 𝑖∈ℕ 0\nL475: \nL476: ,\nL477: \nL478: 𝑎 𝑖→𝑏 𝑖+2; for 𝑗∈ℕ,𝑏 𝑗→𝑎 𝑗+1; and no other comparabilities hold but the necessary ones.\nL479: Then 𝑃 is a locally nite poset satisfying VT and NTC but not SIS.\nL480: \nL481: P r oo f.\nL482: \nL483: The set of covers of Fred is\nL484: \nL485: {𝑎 0, 𝑏 1}\nL486: \nL487: , the set of covers of\nL488: \nL489: 𝑎 𝑖\nL490: \nL491: for\nL492: \nL493: 𝑖≥0\nL494: \nL495: is\nL496: \nL497: {𝑎 𝑖+1, 𝑏 𝑖+2}\nL498: \nL499: , and the\nL500: \nL501: set of covers of\nL502: \nL503: 𝑏 𝑗\nL504: \nL505: for\nL506: \nL507: 𝑗≥1\nL508: \nL509: is\nL510: \nL511: {𝑎 𝑗+1, 𝑏 𝑗+1}\nL512: \nL513: . Thus NTC holds. We have\nL514: \nL515: {𝑎 0, 𝑏 1}→𝑏 2\nL516: \nL51